    { math: '2 · Gel electrophoresis: sorting by size', note: 'Pull DNA through a gel with electricity — short fragments travel faster. The resulting ladder of bands is how you check what you copied and compare who it came from.' },
    { math: '3 · Sequencing: reading the order', note: 'Sanger (1977) read one letter at a time; next-generation machines read millions of fragments in parallel. Human genome: 13 years and $2.7B in 2003 — hours and a few hundred dollars today.' },
    { math: '4 · CRISPR: editing, not just reading', note: 'A bacterial immune system repurposed (2012) into programmable scissors: guide RNA finds the sequence, Cas9 cuts it. Gene therapies are now approved (e.g. sickle-cell, 2023).' },
  ],
};

const pcrCfg = {
  title: 'Worked example: exponential amplification and what it costs',
  steps: [
    { math: 'each cycle: copies = 2ⁿ where n = cycles run', note: 'every double helix becomes two; the math is compounding, the same curve as the exponentials lesson.' },
    { math: '30 cycles: 2³⁰ ≈ 1.07 × 10⁹ copies', note: 'one molecule becomes a billion in about 90 minutes (30 cycles × 3 minutes).' },
    { math: '1 ng of DNA ≈ 2 × 10⁸ copies of a 5 kb target', note: 'so a real reaction starts with abundant templates — the exponential just wins the race for the right one.' },
    { math: 'target 0.1% of the sample → still amplified to purity', note: 'the primer pair defines what gets copied. This is PCR\'s power and its hazard: a stray skin cell in the lab contaminates the reaction.' },
    { math: 'DNA profiling: 20 STR loci, ~10 alleles each', note: 'a random profile match runs ≈ 10⁻¹⁹ and smaller — astronomically unlikely, identical twins excepted. That arithmetic is courtroom evidence.' },
  ],
};

